## Acceptance criteria

Each stated as a property that must be true, with the observation that would
settle it (`skills/core/requirement-interpretation.md`).

| ID | Property that must hold | How it would be shown |
|---|---|---|

**A metric with a tolerance still becomes a binary predicate — over the
*recording*, not over the value.** Forcing a measured quantity into a pass/fail
threshold either invents a number nobody chose or drops the criterion. Write it
as a property of the evidence instead:

> *"Macro-F1 is reported with a 95% confidence interval and the value is
> recorded in evidence; a shortfall against the pre-specified threshold is
> explained rather than hidden."*

That is checkable, cannot be satisfied by hiding a bad result, and does not
require guessing a threshold before the measurement exists. Say
**pre-specified**, not "pre-registered", unless the history really carries a
record predating the measurement.

**A capability writing into a `content_exempt` path carries an acceptance row
saying what actually landed there** and why it is still safe. Exemptions are
declared at contract-authoring time, often before the directory exists, and
their scope grows silently; this is the one point where the declaration is
re-argued against real bytes.

